{
  "id": "Compound::DB00866 - Gene::155",
  "verbalization": "Alprenolol, a beta-blocker, is shown to bind to Adrb1, a subunit of the beta-adrenergic receptor, which is involved in the regulation of adrenergic signaling. This binding is further linked to the participation of Adrb1 in processes such as Norepinephrine Binding and Epinephrine Binding, both of which are part of the broader adrenergic receptor activity. These mechanisms ultimately lead to the participation of Adrb3 in the adrenergic receptor activity pathway. The evidence suggests that Alprenolol exerts its effects through a mechanism involving the beta-adrenergic receptor, with Adrb1 serving as a key intermediate in the interaction with Adrb3. The overlapping pathways and the consistent involvement of Adrb1 in multiple binding and signaling processes support the notion that Alprenolol influences Adrb3 through a shared adrenergic signaling network.",
